  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>Royal and Co., engineers, are in the directories from 1862 to 1945. They are listed in both <ref target="civic?id=Barry%20Street">Barry Street</ref> and <ref target="civic?id=Leicester%20Street">Leicester Street</ref>, at the corner where Barry, Queensberry and Leicester streets meet: in Barry Street from 1862 to 1885 (No. 2 from 1869), and in Leicester Street from 1862 to 1872. The name is Royal, Joseph, and Co. from 1867, and Joseph Royal, engineer, from 1872.</p>
        <p>From 1890 the firm is listed in Leicester Street as axle makers: Royal, Jos., &amp; Co. at No. 109 (1890 and 1895), No. 105 (1900 to 1915), No. 101 (1920), Nos 101–107 (1925 to 1935), Nos 101–113 (1940) and Nos 97–113 (1945), as Royal &amp; Co. from 1915. Joseph Royal is listed at No. 109 in 1900 and 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>William Steel, mason, is at No. 55 Leicester Street, next to the Leicester Street listing, in 1862 and in several years to 1870, and J. Gaynor, boot and shoe manufacturer, at Nos 10–12 Barry Street in 1870 and 1871. <ref target="civic?id=The%20Mills%20hotel">The Mills hotel</ref> is listed at the same corner. Joseph Royal is also listed as a wheelwright and blacksmith in Leicester Street from 1873 to 1885 (see <ref target="civic?id=Blacksmiths">Blacksmiths</ref>).</p>
      </div>
